<section class="page-hero shell">
  <span class="section-number">02 / Process</span>
  <h1>Curiosity first.<br><em>Evidence always.</em></h1>
  <p>LLMs help me navigate large systems and generate adversarial hypotheses. They never get the final vote—runtime proof does.</p>
</section>

<section class="process-list shell">
  <article><span>01</span><div><h2>Understand the promise</h2><p>I start with people, assets, authority, and the boundary that is supposed to hold. A threat model keeps the hunt pointed at consequences that matter.</p></div><small class="hand-note">what must stay true?</small></article>
  <article><span>02</span><div><h2>Trace one thin path</h2><p>I follow one attacker-controlled source through its guards and transformations to one sensitive sink. Focus makes assumptions visible.</p></div><small class="hand-note">source → sink</small></article>
  <article><span>03</span><div><h2>Build the cheapest oracle</h2><p>Exact artifacts, positive controls, direct-boundary probes, and guarded negatives separate product behavior from a persuasive-looking coincidence.</p></div><small class="hand-note">try to kill the idea</small></article>
  <article><span>04</span><div><h2>Prove the human impact</h2><p>A primitive becomes a finding only when the attacker gains something and the victim demonstrably loses something. Limitations belong in the report.</p></div><small class="hand-note">proof, not theatre</small></article>
  <article><span>05</span><div><h2>Disclose responsibly</h2><p>I package a reproducible path, evidence, root cause, and a practical fix—then give the vendor room to investigate before anything becomes public.</p></div><small class="hand-note">make fixing easier</small></article>
</section>

<section class="paper-quote">
  <div class="shell"><p>“The model can suggest where to look.<br><em>The evidence decides what is real.</em>”</p></div>
</section>
